<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boran</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/632">632 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boran was a queen of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> who briefly ruled in the early 630s CE, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a>. She was one of about a dozen claimants to the throne in the years after Khosrow's deposition in 628, including another queen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azarmidokht">Azarmidokht</a>. Neither could command the loyalty of the great noble houses or the army, and the political disintegration left the empire exposed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boran.jpg" alt="Boran" width="320" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_coin_of_Boran,_daughter_of_Khusrau_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Boran, also spelled Buran, was the daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-ii">Khosrow II</a> and the Byzantine princess Maria. Her father was overthrown and executed in 628 by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kavad-ii">Kavad II</a>, who had all Boran's brothers and half-brothers killed, including the heir Mardanshah. According to one chronicle, Boran was also Kavad II's wife, an instance of the Zoroastrian practice of close-kin marriage. Kavad II died some months later in an epidemic known as the Plague of Sheroe, and his young son became Ardashir III. The Sasanian civil war of 628 to 632 followed, in which powerful nobles took autonomy, and the Persian (Parsig) and Parthian (Pahlav) noble families renewed their hostility.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The Pahlav leader Farrukh Hormizd began to support Boran, who minted coins in Pahlav areas. On 27 April 630 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz">Shahrbaraz</a> killed Ardashir III and was himself murdered about forty days later by Farrukh Hormizd, who helped Boran take the throne in late June 630. She was the first queen to rule the empire, and her accession probably owed much to her being, with her sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azarmidokht">Azarmidokht</a>, the only legitimate heir able to rule. She made Farrukh Hormizd chief minister and worked for stability through just laws, repair of infrastructure, lower taxes and coinage. Her coins were minted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pars">Pars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khuzestan">Khuzestan</a>, Media and Abarshahr, and no objection to her sex is recorded.</p><p>She was soon deposed in favour of Shapur-i Shahrvaraz and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azarmidokht">Azarmidokht</a>, who had Farrukh Hormizd killed. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam-farrokhzad">Rostam Farrokhzad</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, had Azarmidokht blinded and killed, and restored Boran in June 631. She gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a> overall power, and sent an embassy led by the catholicos Ishoyahb II to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>, which was well received.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A revolt broke out in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a> the following year, led by Parsig who disliked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a>'s regency, and Boran was killed, presumably strangled by Piruz Khosrow. Rostam and Piruz Khosrow then agreed to place her nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-iii">Yazdegerd III</a> on the throne, ending the civil war. According to al-Tabari, she reigned sixteen months in all. She was the penultimate Sasanian ruler.</p><p>Her coins are the only Sasanian coins with a queen's portrait. They return to her father's design and bear the legend Boran, restorer of the race of Gods, a claim to divine descent not used since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-ii">Shapur II</a> in the 4th century. She also issued bronze coins, and one gold issue is known.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A succession crisis followed in which perhaps a dozen rulers claimed the throne within four years, among them the queens <b>Boran</b> and Azarmidokht, none of whom could command the loyalty of the great noble houses or the army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=622" aria-label="Ctesiphon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5691 962 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Boran"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6141 1232h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6141 1232h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=33.09,44.58&amp;year=622">What was here in 622 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
